#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fpms-npud - the FPMS NPU inference daemon.

Owns the RKNN runtime. Nothing else in this image may construct an RKNNLite.

Usage:
    fpms-npud              run in the foreground (systemd Type=simple)
    fpms-npud --help       this text


WHY THIS PROCESS EXISTS
=======================
Today inference runs INSIDE fpms_rover_agent.py's camera_loop, and the entire
NPU block - import, load_rknn, init_runtime, labels - is wrapped in ONE
try/except that logs

    NPU unavailable (...); streaming without detection

exactly once and then carries on forever (fpms_rover_agent.py, the
`except Exception as e:` at the end of the NPU setup block). Two separate
defects come out of that shape, and this daemon exists to remove both:

  1. COUPLING. `rknn.inference()` is called on the camera thread, between
     `cap.read()` and the JPEG encode. That same thread also runs detect_fire()
     - the flame screen this rover is FOR. A slow or wedged NPU call therefore
     stalls the frame pump AND the fire detector. bench_npu.py measures the
     inference step at ~31 ms of a ~? ms frame budget on the old Pi with
     yolov8n; that is fine right up until the moment it is not, and there is no
     bound on how long a librknnrt call can take when the driver is unhappy.

  2. SILENCE. One log line, once, at startup, in a journal nobody is reading -
     and then a rover that streams video, reports every unit `active`, and
     detects nothing. NPU_SPEC.md calls this "the single worst failure mode in
     the project, because it is indistinguishable from success at every level an
     operator normally checks." A daemon whose ONLY job is inference can make
     that state a first-class, persistent, published fault instead of a
     scrollback entry.

So: one process, one runtime, one worker thread, a socket, and a health topic
that never stops telling the truth.

WHAT THIS DAEMON DOES NOT OWN
  - labelling. The daemon returns boxes/scores/classes, exactly the contract of
    fpms_yolo26_npu.decode(). COCO_NAMES, custom_labels.json, the wildlife /
    vegetation / person "kind" mapping and the drawing all stay in the agent.
    Splitting presentation out of the runtime is the point.
  - core mask POLICY. Agent 4 (fpms-npu-tune) decides what the mask should be
    and writes it to FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK. This file owns the MECHANISM: reading
    that variable, translating it to an RKNNLite constant, applying it, failing
    loudly if it cannot, and publishing what is actually in force.
  - model provenance. Agent 3 (fpms-model-verify, /etc/fpms/models.json) is the
    authority on which sha256 is the right one. This file reads that registry
    if it exists and refuses on a CONTRADICTION, but it does not write it.
  - the selftest. Agent 7 (fpms-npu-selftest) decides pass/fail for an operator.


================================================================================
WIRE FORMAT  -  unix SOCK_STREAM at $FPMS_NPU_SOCKET (default /run/fpms/npud.sock)
================================================================================
Length-prefixed, with a magic and a version byte in every frame, in BOTH
directions. That is deliberate: a client built against protocol 1 talking to a
daemon speaking protocol 2 must get a NAMED refusal, not a plausible-looking
garbled parse. This project has been bitten repeatedly by failures that look
like success; a silently misread box array would be exactly that again.

Header, 12 bytes, network byte order, struct "!4sBBBBI":

    off  size  field
    ---  ----  --------------------------------------------------------------
      0     4  magic     b"FNPU"
      4     1  version   protocol version, currently 1
      5     1  msgtype   see below
      6     1  flags     reserved, MUST be 0 (a non-zero value is refused, so
                         that adding a flag later cannot be silently ignored by
                         an old daemon)
      7     1  fmt       payload image format, see below
      8     4  length    payload byte count, uint32 BE, <= 4 MiB

msgtype - requests have the high bit CLEAR, responses have it SET, so a frame
read off the wrong end of the socket is immediately recognisable:

    0x01  INFER          client -> daemon, one frame to run
    0x02  HEALTH         client -> daemon, "what is your state", no image
    0x81  RESULT         daemon -> client, boxes (JSON)
    0x82  UNAVAILABLE    daemon -> client, NAMED fault, NEVER any boxes
    0x83  HEALTH_OK      daemon -> client, health snapshot (JSON)
    0x84  PROTOCOL_ERROR daemon -> client, then the connection is closed
    0x85  DROPPED        daemon -> client, this frame was superseded by a newer
                         one and was never run. NOT a fault - it is backpressure
                         working as designed. See "QUEUE" below.

fmt:

    0x00  NONE           no image bytes (HEALTH, and every response)
    0x01  RGB888_640     the image is ALREADY 640x640x3 uint8 RGB, C-contiguous,
                         HWC, exactly 1 228 800 bytes.

  Only one image format is defined, on purpose. The agent already does
  `cv2.resize(frame, (640, 640))` + `cvtColor(BGR2RGB)` on its own thread and
  bench_npu.py measures that preprocess step as a small fraction of the
  inference step, so moving it across the socket would buy nothing and would
  put an image-format negotiation - a thing to get wrong - on the hot path.
  fmt 0x02 is RESERVED for "raw BGR at native size, daemon resizes", if
  measurement ever justifies it.

INFER payload (fmt 0x01):

    !I    meta_len
    ...   meta_len bytes of UTF-8 JSON
    ...   the image bytes (1 228 800 for fmt 0x01)

  meta keys:
    "h", "w"   REQUIRED. Height/width of the ORIGINAL camera frame, in pixels.
               The decoder rescales boxes back into this space. Because the
               agent preprocesses with a plain stretch resize and NOT a
               letterbox, that rescale is an INDEPENDENT per-axis ratio - see
               fpms_yolo26_npu.decode(). If the agent's preprocess ever becomes
               a letterbox, both that decoder AND this contract change.
    "seq"      optional int, echoed back. Lets a client match replies to frames.
    "conf"     optional float, per-request confidence threshold override.
    "iou"      optional float, per-request NMS IoU override.

RESULT payload: UTF-8 JSON

    {"seq": 41, "n": 2,
     "boxes":  [[x1,y1,x2,y2], ...],   floats, ORIGINAL-frame pixels
     "scores": [0.71, ...],
     "classes":[0, 58, ...],           COCO ids
     "infer_ms": 31.4,                 the rknn.inference() call alone
     "decode_ms": 3.1,
     "queue_ms": 0.2,                  time the job waited before running
     "model_sha256": "...",            so a client can detect a hot model swap
     "core_mask": "0_1_2"}

UNAVAILABLE payload: UTF-8 JSON

    {"seq": 41, "fault": "model_missing", "detail": "...",
     "since": 1765200000.0, "required": true}

  There is deliberately no "boxes": [] in an UNAVAILABLE reply. An empty box
  list means "the NPU ran and saw nothing", which is a completely different
  statement from "there is no NPU", and a client that cannot tell them apart
  will draw the wrong conclusion. NPU_SPEC.md design rule 3: never claim a
  detection you cannot substantiate - and "nothing is there" is a claim.

DROPPED payload: {"seq": 41, "reason": "superseded", "queue_ms": 812.0}

PROTOCOL_ERROR payload: {"error": "...", "daemon_version": 1}


CLIENT REFERENCE  (for whoever edits fpms_rover_agent.py - that file is not
mine to change, so the exact call sequence is written down here instead):

    import json, socket, struct
    HDR = struct.Struct("!4sBBBBI")

    s = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
    s.settimeout(1.0)                       # NEVER block the camera thread
    s.connect(os.environ.get("FPMS_NPU_SOCKET", "/run/fpms/npud.sock"))

    img = cv2.cvtColor(cv2.resize(frame, (640, 640)), cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)
    meta = json.dumps({"h": frame.shape[0], "w": frame.shape[1],
                       "seq": frame_index}).encode()
    blob = struct.pack("!I", len(meta)) + meta + img.tobytes()
    s.sendall(HDR.pack(b"FNPU", 1, 0x01, 0, 0x01, len(blob)) + blob)

    magic, ver, mt, flags, fmt, n = HDR.unpack(recv_exact(s, 12))
    body = json.loads(recv_exact(s, n))
    if mt == 0x81:   use body["boxes"] / body["scores"] / body["classes"]
    elif mt == 0x85: KEEP THE PREVIOUS BOXES - the frame was skipped, not blind
    elif mt == 0x82: PUBLISH "npu": false and body["fault"] - do NOT go quiet
    else:            protocol/daemon mismatch; treat as 0x82

    Keep the connection open across frames; one connect per frame works but
    wastes a syscall round trip. On ANY socket error, close, set npu=false, and
    reconnect lazily on the next inferred frame with a backoff. The agent must
    never die, block, or fall silent because this daemon is down.


================================================================================
QUEUE  -  bounded, DROP-OLDEST
================================================================================
Depth is FPMS_NPU_QUEUE, default 1.

The camera ships at FPMS_CAMERA_FPS=6 with FPMS_INFER_EVERY=2, so the offered
load is ~3 inferences/second - roughly a 333 ms budget against a ~31 ms
measured inference. In a healthy system this queue is ALWAYS empty. Anything
sitting in it means the NPU is already behind, and the only question is what to
throw away.

DROP-OLDEST, and here is why it is not arguable for this workload:

  - The value of a detection decays with age, fast. This is a moving rover
    looking for fire. A box drawn where the flame was 4 seconds ago is not
    "stale but useful", it is WRONG - it points the operator at the wrong part
    of the frame, and it is wrong in a way that still looks like a working
    detector.
  - Every queued frame is strictly less current than the one behind it. There
    is no information in an old frame that the new frame does not have more of.
  - Drop-newest would mean that under load the daemon serves an ever-older
    view while continuously discarding the only frames that matter. That is
    the failure mode of a video pipeline that "keeps up" on paper and lags in
    reality, and this project has already been burned by exactly that shape:
    LiDAR scans were never DROPPED behind camera frames on the shared paho
    client, they simply arrived LATE - "the hardest version of this failure to
    see" (fpms-rover-agent.service). Late is worse than absent when the
    consumer cannot tell the difference.

The evicted job is answered immediately with DROPPED. It is never left to time
out: a client blocked on a reply that will never come is a stalled camera
thread, which is the thing this daemon was built to prevent.

Depth 1 rather than 0 (i.e. reject-if-busy) so that a single frame arriving
during an in-flight inference still gets run rather than discarded - at 3 fps
that is the common case, not the overload case.


================================================================================
TIMEOUTS  -  what is enforceable, and what is not. Read this before trusting it.
================================================================================
FPMS_NPU_MAX_LATENCY_MS is the per-inference budget. There are three layers and
they enforce genuinely different amounts:

  1. CLIENT DEADLINE - fully enforceable, and it is the one that matters.
     The connection thread waits on the job's completion Event with a timeout of
     2x the budget. When it expires the client gets UNAVAILABLE/"timeout" and
     gets its thread back. THE CAMERA THREAD IS NEVER BLOCKED BY A WEDGED NPU.
     That property does not depend on being able to cancel anything, only on
     the inference living in a different process from the camera - which is the
     entire architectural point of this daemon.

     The deadline is deliberately 2x, not 1x. If the client deadline equalled
     the worker's budget, every inference that was slow-but-successful would be
     reported to the client as a timeout while the worker recorded it as a
     success - two contradictory stories about the same call, and the telemetry
     would be the one that looked fine.

  2. WORKER BUDGET - detection only, NOT cancellation.
     `RKNNLite.inference()` is a blocking call into librknnrt.so. It has no
     cancel, no deadline argument, and no thread-safe abort, and it does not
     return to the interpreter until the driver returns. THERE IS NO WAY TO
     INTERRUPT IT FROM PYTHON. A signal will not do it (Python only runs the
     handler between bytecodes, and we are not executing bytecode), and
     Thread.join(timeout) leaves the thread running. So the worker can only
     measure the call AFTER it returns and count it as a fault - which it does:
     over-budget calls increment consec_slow, publish npu_fault/"inference_slow",
     and after FPMS_NPU_REINIT_AFTER of them trigger a runtime re-init.

  3. HARD TIMEOUT - a process-level kill, because that is the only cancellation
     primitive the OS actually gives us here.
     If a call has been in flight for longer than FPMS_NPU_HARD_TIMEOUT_MS
     (default 10x the budget) the watchdog declares the runtime wedged,
     publishes npu_fault/"runtime_wedged" at QoS 1, gives MQTT a moment to
     flush, and calls os._exit(3). Not sys.exit() - that raises SystemExit in
     the calling thread and the interpreter would then wait forever to join the
     stuck worker. Not rknn.release() either: releasing a context whose C call
     has not returned is undefined and could hang or segfault the same process
     we are trying to get out of.

     systemd restarts the unit (Restart=always, RestartSec=3), which gives a
     fresh process, a fresh librknnrt, and a fresh driver context. RECOVERY
     FROM A WEDGED NPU IS A RESTART. Saying that in a comment is more honest
     than pretending a threading construct can undo it.

     UNMEASURED: nobody has ever seen an rknpu call wedge on this board,
     because the board has not arrived. 10x the budget is a bound chosen to be
     far outside any plausible thermal-throttle excursion, not a measurement.
     MEASURE ME once sustained load numbers exist (agent 7's bench).


================================================================================
FAULT PUBLISHING  -  edge-triggered, with a cooldown. This is not optional.
================================================================================
A past FPMS bug emailed 330 obstacle events and exhausted the Resend daily
quota, so a REAL FIRE ALERT COULD NOT SEND (CLAUDE.md, "Hard-won constraints";
cloud/HANDOFF.md item 10). An alert path that fires per-occurrence does not
merely spam - it destroys the alerting channel for the one event the whole
product exists to report.

So npu_fault is published on the RISING EDGE of a named fault only, then at
most once per FPMS_NPU_FAULT_REPEAT_S (default 300) while it persists, and
npu_recovered once on the falling edge. Changing the fault NAME counts as a new
edge - a model_missing that becomes a runtime_wedged is different news - but
the same name repeating does not.

Continuous state lives on telemetry/npu at QoS 0 instead, where a dashboard can
poll it as often as it likes and it costs no quota anywhere. The rule is:
telemetry describes, events interrupt.


================================================================================
NEVER RUN ON HARDWARE
================================================================================
The Orange Pi 5B has not arrived. NOTHING in this file has executed against a
real rknpu driver, a real librknnrt.so, or a real .rknn model. Everything below
that touches RKNNLite is written against Rockchip's documented API and against
the call sequence in bench_npu.py and fpms_rover_agent.py (load_rknn ->
init_runtime -> inference -> release), which DID run on the old Pi.

Specifically unconfirmed, with the command that confirms each:
  - the device node name              ls -l /dev/rknpu* /dev/dri/renderD*
  - the group that owns it            stat -c '%U %G %a' /dev/rknpu
  - the driver version string         cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version
  - whether debugfs is readable as `ubuntu` at all (it is root-only by default,
    so the driver version will very likely publish as null - that is expected,
    not a fault)
  - whether init_runtime() on this wheel accepts core_mask as a keyword
  - whether THIS model was built for multi-core and will accept 0_1_2
  - every latency number quoted anywhere in this file
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import collections
import hashlib
import json
import math
import os
import signal
import socket
import struct
import sys
import threading
import time

import numpy as np
from paho.mqtt.client import Client, CallbackAPIVersion

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- config ---
#
# Same loader shape as fpms_rover_agent.py / fpms-selftest, deliberately: this
# stack has one configuration point and adding a second parsing dialect for it
# is how the two halves of a setting drift apart.
#
# UNLIKE the agent, os.environ wins over the file here. config.env.example
# documents that the services disagree about this (cored overlays environ on
# top, the agent reads the file only) - and for THIS daemon the systemd unit is
# where the NPU defaults live, so the unit's Environment= block must be able to
# set them. The unit still puts EnvironmentFile= FIRST, so an operator editing
# config.env can override the unit's defaults, which is the intended precedence.


def load_config(path="/etc/fpms/config.env"):
    cfg = {}
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            for line in f:
                line = line.strip()
                if line and not line.startswith("#") and "=" in line:
                    k, v = line.split("=", 1)
                    cfg[k.strip()] = v.strip().strip('"').strip("'")
    except OSError:
        # Absent config.env is not fatal here, but it IS the reason every FPMS
        # service silently retries against the wrong broker forever. Logged in
        # main() rather than swallowed.
        pass
    cfg.update({k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k.startswith("FPMS_")})
    return cfg


CFG = load_config()


def _s(key, default):
    return str(CFG.get(key, default)).strip()


def _i(key, default):
    try:
        return int(float(_s(key, default)))
    except ValueError:
        return int(float(default))


def _f(key, default):
    try:
        return float(_s(key, default))
    except ValueError:
        return float(default)


THING = _s("FPMS_THING_NAME", "rover2")
BROKER = _s("FPMS_MQTT_HOST", "127.0.0.1")
PORT = _i("FPMS_MQTT_PORT", "1883")
USER = _s("FPMS_MQTT_USER", "")
PASS = _s("FPMS_MQTT_PASS", "")

SOCKET_PATH = _s("FPMS_NPU_SOCKET", "/run/fpms/npud.sock")
YOLO_DIR = _s("FPMS_YOLO_DIR", "/home/ubuntu/yolo")
YOLO_VARIANT = _s("FPMS_YOLO_VARIANT", "v26").lower()

# The core mask. "0" | "0_1" | "0_1_2" | "auto".
#
# NPU_SPEC.md: init_runtime() with NO core_mask uses core 0 ONLY, which leaves
# two thirds of a 6 TOPS 3-core NPU idle - that is the recorded defect this
# layer exists to close, so the DEFAULT here is 0_1_2 rather than a
# reproduction of the defect.
#
# MEASURE ME: whether 0_1_2 actually beats 0 for ONE 640x640 yolo26n graph is
# UNMEASURED and is not obvious. Rockchip's multi-core modes trade per-inference
# latency against aggregate throughput differently depending on how the model
# was built, and NPU_SPEC.md explicitly flags "single-core per-model with three
# models in parallel, versus one model across all three" as a real open
# decision. Agent 4 (fpms-npu-tune) owns resolving it; this daemon only carries
# out whatever FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK says and PUBLISHES what is actually in force,
# so the answer is never inferred from a default nobody checked.
CORE_MASK_NAME = _s("FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK", "0_1_2").lower()

# Per-inference budget. UNMEASURED as a THRESHOLD.
#
# The only latency evidence in this repo is bench_npu.py's ~31 ms for the
# inference step, on the OLD Pi, with yolov8n, on a FIRST-inference-style short
# run. NPU_SPEC.md: "Measure sustained latency, not a first-inference number" -
# RK3588 throttles hard and silently under sustained load. 400 ms is chosen to
# sit far above any plausible healthy value INCLUDING a throttled one, so that
# crossing it means something is genuinely wrong rather than merely warm. It is
# not a performance target. Agent 7's bench produces the real number.
MAX_LATENCY_MS = _f("FPMS_NPU_MAX_LATENCY_MS", "400")
HARD_TIMEOUT_MS = _f("FPMS_NPU_HARD_TIMEOUT_MS", str(MAX_LATENCY_MS * 10))

QUEUE_DEPTH = max(1, _i("FPMS_NPU_QUEUE", "1"))
MAX_CLIENTS = max(1, _i("FPMS_NPU_MAX_CLIENTS", "8"))
REINIT_AFTER = max(1, _i("FPMS_NPU_REINIT_AFTER", "3"))
RELOAD_BACKOFF_S = _f("FPMS_NPU_RELOAD_BACKOFF_S", "30")

TELEMETRY_S = _f("FPMS_NPU_TELEMETRY_S", "5")
FAULT_REPEAT_S = _f("FPMS_NPU_FAULT_REPEAT_S", "300")

# Is a working NPU a requirement of this deployment, or a nice-to-have?
#
# This does NOT decide whether the daemon exits - it never exits on a missing
# NPU. NPU_SPEC.md requires the opposite: "If the NPU is unavailable the daemon
# must stay up and keep saying so, so the dashboard shows a persistent fault
# rather than silence." A crash-looping unit publishes nothing and is exactly
# the silence we are trying to remove.
#
# What it decides is whether npu_fault carries alert:true (operator-visible,
# reaches the cloud alert path) or alert:false (bench/dev box knowingly running
# without detection). Default 1: on a fire-detection rover, no detector is an
# alert.
REQUIRED = _s("FPMS_NPU_REQUIRED", "1") not in ("0", "false", "no", "")

MODELS_JSON = "/etc/fpms/models.json"            # agent 3 owns this
NPU_VERSIONS_JSON = "/etc/fpms/npu-versions.json"  # agent 1 owns this
DRIVER_VERSION_PATH = "/sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version"

RUNNING = threading.Event()
RUNNING.set()
STARTED = time.time()

# ------------------------------------------------------------- protocol ---

MAGIC = b"FNPU"
PROTO_VERSION = 1
HEADER = struct.Struct("!4sBBBBI")
HEADER_LEN = HEADER.size                       # 12

MSG_INFER = 0x01
MSG_HEALTH = 0x02
MSG_RESULT = 0x81
MSG_UNAVAILABLE = 0x82
MSG_HEALTH_OK = 0x83
MSG_PROTOCOL_ERROR = 0x84
MSG_DROPPED = 0x85

FMT_NONE = 0x00
FMT_RGB888_640 = 0x01

INPUT_SIZE = 640
IMAGE_BYTES = INPUT_SIZE * INPUT_SIZE * 3      # 1 228 800

# A length-prefixed protocol with no cap is a memory exhaustion primitive for
# anyone who can open the socket. 4 MiB is ~3.4x the only legal image size, so
# it accommodates a future fmt without accommodating an attack.
MAX_PAYLOAD = 4 * 1024 * 1024


def log(*a):
    print(time.strftime("%H:%M:%S"), *a, flush=True)


# ------------------------------------------------------------- MQTT bus ---


class Bus:
    """Thin MQTT wrapper. Publishing must never raise into the worker."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.client = Client(client_id=f"{THING}-npud",
                             callback_api_version=CallbackAPIVersion.VERSION2)
        if USER:
            self.client.username_pw_set(USER, PASS or None)
        self.client.on_connect = self._on_connect
        # A last will, for the same reason the agent has one: if this process
        # dies the dashboard must learn it from the broker rather than by
        # noticing that telemetry stopped. "Telemetry stopped" is precisely the
        # silence this daemon exists to eliminate, and it must not be the way
        # its own death is announced.
        self.client.will_set(
            f"fpms/{THING}/events/npu_fault",
            json.dumps({"thing": THING, "component": "npu", "alert": REQUIRED,
                        "fault": "daemon_offline",
                        "error": "fpms-npud disconnected without a clean stop; "
                                 "no inference is being served",
                        "detection_available": False}),
            qos=1, retain=False)
        self.connected = False

    def start(self):
        # loop_start() gives paho its own network thread. connect_async() means
        # a broker that is not up yet does not block the socket from listening -
        # clients still get an honest UNAVAILABLE instead of ECONNREFUSED.
        try:
            self.client.connect_async(BROKER, PORT, keepalive=30)
            self.client.loop_start()
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            log(f"MQTT setup failed ({e}); telemetry will retry in the background")

    def _on_connect(self, client, _u, _f, reason_code, _p=None):
        self.connected = True
        log(f"MQTT connected to {BROKER}:{PORT} ({reason_code})")
        self.publish("events/npu_online", {
            "component": "npu",
            "socket": SOCKET_PATH,
            "protocol": PROTO_VERSION,
            "core_mask_requested": CORE_MASK_NAME,
            "required": REQUIRED,
        }, qos=1)

    def publish(self, suffix, payload, qos=0):
        """Returns True only if the message was actually handed to paho.

        The return value is load-bearing for the fault path: an edge-triggered
        alert that is "sent" into a disconnected client has consumed its edge
        and will not be retried for a whole cooldown. See set_fault().
        """
        try:
            payload.setdefault("ts", time.time())
            payload.setdefault("thing", THING)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
            pass
        if not self.connected:
            return False
        try:
            self.client.publish(f"fpms/{THING}/{suffix}",
                                json.dumps(payload), qos=qos)
            return True
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            log(f"publish failed on {suffix}: {e}")
            return False


BUS = Bus()


# --------------------------------------------------------------- metrics ---


class Metrics:
    """Latency percentiles, rates and counters. Cheap, lock-guarded."""

    # 256 samples at ~3/s is ~85 s of history: long enough to show a thermal
    # excursion, short enough that a percentile still describes NOW.
    WINDOW = 256
    RATE_WINDOW_S = 30.0

    def __init__(self):
        self.lock = threading.Lock()
        self.lat = collections.deque(maxlen=self.WINDOW)
        self.done_at = collections.deque()
        self.total = 0
        self.drops = 0
        self.timeouts = 0
        self.slow = 0
        self.errors = 0
        self.consec_fail = 0
        self.consec_slow = 0
        self.reinits = 0
        self.proto_errors = 0

    def record(self, ms, ok):
        now = time.monotonic()
        with self.lock:
            if ok:
                self.lat.append(ms)
                self.total += 1
                self.done_at.append(now)
                cutoff = now - self.RATE_WINDOW_S
                while self.done_at and self.done_at[0] < cutoff:
                    self.done_at.popleft()

    @staticmethod
    def _pct(vals, q):
        """Nearest-rank percentile - an OBSERVED latency, never an interpolated one.

        np.percentile's default linear interpolation would report a value that
        no inference ever took. For a latency bound that is used to decide
        whether the NPU is healthy, "the 90th slowest thing that actually
        happened" is the honest answer; a number between two samples is a
        number the hardware never produced.
        """
        if not vals:
            return None
        k = int(math.ceil(q * len(vals))) - 1
        return round(vals[max(0, min(k, len(vals) - 1))], 2)

    def snapshot(self):
        with self.lock:
            vals = sorted(self.lat)
            now = time.monotonic()
            cutoff = now - self.RATE_WINDOW_S
            while self.done_at and self.done_at[0] < cutoff:
                self.done_at.popleft()
            n_recent = len(self.done_at)
            return {
                "samples": len(vals),
                "p50_ms": self._pct(vals, 0.50),
                "p90_ms": self._pct(vals, 0.90),
                "max_ms": round(vals[-1], 2) if vals else None,
                "infer_per_s": round(n_recent / self.RATE_WINDOW_S, 2),
                "total": self.total,
                "drops": self.drops,
                "timeouts": self.timeouts,
                "slow": self.slow,
                "errors": self.errors,
                "consecutive_failures": self.consec_fail,
                "consecutive_slow": self.consec_slow,
                "reinits": self.reinits,
                "protocol_errors": self.proto_errors,
            }


METRICS = Metrics()


# ----------------------------------------------------------- fault state ---
#
# One named fault at a time, edge-triggered. See the FAULT PUBLISHING section
# in the module docstring for why the cooldown is load-bearing.

FAULT = {"name": "loading", "detail": "runtime not loaded yet",
         "since": time.time(), "last_sent": 0.0}
FAULT_LOCK = threading.Lock()


def set_fault(name, detail):
    """Declare a named fault. Publishes only on a rising edge or after cooldown."""
    now = time.time()
    with FAULT_LOCK:
        changed = FAULT["name"] != name
        if changed:
            FAULT.update({"name": name, "detail": detail,
                          "since": now, "last_sent": 0.0})
        else:
            FAULT["detail"] = detail
        due = changed or (now - FAULT["last_sent"] >= FAULT_REPEAT_S)
        if not due:
            return
        payload = {"component": "npu", "alert": REQUIRED, "fault": name,
                   "error": detail, "since": FAULT["since"],
                   "detection_available": False,
                   "required": REQUIRED,
                   "repeat": not changed}
    if changed:
        log(f"FAULT {name}: {detail}")

    # last_sent advances ONLY on a publish that actually left. This daemon
    # starts alongside mosquitto and its first fault - model_missing,
    # runtime_import - typically rises seconds before the broker connection
    # completes. Marking the edge as spent on a publish that went nowhere would
    # swallow the very first and most important alert for a full
    # FPMS_NPU_FAULT_REPEAT_S (default five minutes), which is the exact
    # "silence that looks like health" this file exists to abolish. Retrying
    # here does not reopen the 330-email hole either: the retry is driven by
    # the SAME cooldown clock, so an unpublishable fault is attempted at most
    # once per set_fault() call and the cooldown starts when it lands.
    if BUS.publish("events/npu_fault", payload, qos=1):
        with FAULT_LOCK:
            if FAULT["name"] == name:
                FAULT["last_sent"] = time.time()


def clear_fault():
    """Falling edge. Published once, never repeated."""
    with FAULT_LOCK:
        if FAULT["name"] is None:
            return
        was, since = FAULT["name"], FAULT["since"]
        FAULT.update({"name": None, "detail": "", "since": time.time(),
                      "last_sent": 0.0})
    log(f"recovered from fault '{was}'")
    BUS.publish("events/npu_recovered", {
        "component": "npu", "alert": False, "cleared": was,
        "outage_s": round(time.time() - since, 1),
        "detection_available": True,
    }, qos=1)


def current_fault():
    with FAULT_LOCK:
        if FAULT["name"] is None:
            return None
        return {"fault": FAULT["name"], "detail": FAULT["detail"],
                "since": FAULT["since"]}


# ---------------------------------------------------------- the runtime ---


def sha256_file(path):
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    with open(path, "rb") as fh:
        for chunk in iter(lambda: fh.read(1 << 20), b""):
            h.update(chunk)
    return h.hexdigest()


def read_text(path):
    """Best effort. debugfs is root-only by default and we run as ubuntu."""
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            return fh.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        return None


def registry_sha(model_path):
    """Expected sha256 from agent 3's /etc/fpms/models.json, or a reason why not.

    Returns (sha_or_None, note). THREE outcomes, and collapsing them would lose
    the whole point of the registry:

      a str   - the registry states a hash. A difference is a REFUSAL.
      None + "declared UNKNOWN"  - the registry has an entry for this file and
                                   explicitly sets sha256: null. Agent 3's
                                   _readme is emphatic that this is a declared,
                                   checkable state and not a shrug: the .rknn
                                   has never been in git and has never been
                                   hashed, and a fabricated hash would make
                                   fpms-model-verify FAIL on the one correct
                                   file the project owns.
      None + anything else       - no registry, no entry, or a file we cannot
                                   parse. Reported as such. A registry we
                                   cannot read must never silently become a
                                   registry that agrees with us.

    Only the first outcome can refuse. The other two set sha_state=unverified,
    which is published in telemetry and is agent 7's business, not a reason to
    stop serving inference that is probably fine.
    """
    if not os.path.exists(MODELS_JSON):
        return None, "registry absent (agent 3's fpms-model-verify not installed)"
    try:
        with open(MODELS_JSON) as fh:
            doc = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        return None, f"registry unreadable: {e}"

    base = os.path.basename(model_path)

    def names_this_file(entry):
        f = entry.get("file")
        return isinstance(f, str) and (f == model_path or os.path.basename(f) == base)

    # The documented schema first: {"models": {"<logical>": {"file":..., "sha256":...}}}
    models = doc.get("models")
    if isinstance(models, dict):
        for logical, entry in models.items():
            if not isinstance(entry, dict) or not names_this_file(entry):
                continue
            sha = entry.get("sha256")
            if isinstance(sha, str) and sha.strip():
                return sha.strip().lower(), f"registry entry '{logical}'"
            status = entry.get("sha256_status") or "no sha256_status recorded"
            return None, (f"registry entry '{logical}' declares sha256 UNKNOWN: "
                          f"{status}")

    # Fall back to a structural walk, in case the schema moves under us. Better
    # to find a stated hash in an unexpected place than to miss a contradiction.
    stack = [doc]
    while stack:
        node = stack.pop()
        if isinstance(node, dict):
            vals = {v for v in node.values() if isinstance(v, str)}
            if (base in vals or model_path in vals) \
                    and isinstance(node.get("sha256"), str) and node["sha256"].strip():
                return node["sha256"].strip().lower(), "registry (unexpected schema)"
            stack.extend(node.values())
        elif isinstance(node, list):
            stack.extend(node)
    return None, f"registry present but states no sha256 for {base}"


class Runtime:
    """Everything that touches RKNNLite. Single-threaded by construction.

    Only the worker thread ever calls load()/infer()/release(). The watchdog
    reads `inflight_started` and nothing else. That is not an accident: the
    RKNN context is not documented as thread-safe, and serialising access
    through one worker is both the safety property and the reason a bounded
    queue is meaningful.
    """

    def __init__(self):
        self.rknn = None
        self.decode = None
        self.model_path = None
        self.model_sha = None
        self.model_bytes = 0
        self.core_mask_name = CORE_MASK_NAME
        self.core_mask_fallback = False
        self.sha_state = "unverified"
        self.sha_note = ""
        self.loaded = False
        self.probe_ms = None
        # Written by the worker immediately before rknn.inference() and cleared
        # immediately after. A float monotonic timestamp, or 0.0. Read by the
        # watchdog. Assignment of a float is atomic under the GIL, so no lock is
        # needed and taking one here would be worse - the watchdog must never be
        # able to block behind the thread it is watching.
        self.inflight_started = 0.0
        self.inflight_seq = 0

    # -- core mask ---------------------------------------------------------

    def resolve_core_mask(self, rknnlite_cls):
        """Map FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK onto an RKNNLite constant.

        getattr rather than a direct attribute reference: the aarch64
        rknn-toolkit-lite2 wheel is version-coupled to librknnrt.so and to the
        kernel driver (NPU_SPEC.md section 1), and an older wheel may simply not
        define NPU_CORE_0_1_2. That must be a NAMED refusal, not an
        AttributeError inside a try/except that logs once and carries on -
        which is the exact failure shape this daemon replaces.
        """
        table = {
            "0": "NPU_CORE_0",
            "0_1": "NPU_CORE_0_1",
            "0_1_2": "NPU_CORE_0_1_2",
            "auto": "NPU_CORE_AUTO",
        }
        attr = table.get(self.core_mask_name)
        if attr is None:
            raise ValueError(
                f"FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK={self.core_mask_name!r} is not one of "
                f"{sorted(table)}")
        val = getattr(rknnlite_cls, attr, None)
        if val is None:
            raise ValueError(
                f"this rknn-toolkit-lite2 wheel has no {attr}; the wheel, "
                f"librknnrt.so and the kernel rknpu driver must be "
                f"version-matched (see /etc/fpms/npu-versions.json)")
        return val

    # -- load --------------------------------------------------------------

    def load(self):
        """Load model + runtime. Returns True on success, sets a fault on failure."""
        self.release()

        # 1. The decode module. It lives next to the model in FPMS_YOLO_DIR and
        #    is imported, not reimplemented: fpms_yolo26_npu.decode() carries
        #    the stretch-vs-letterbox rescale, the "already sigmoided, do not
        #    sigmoid again" rule and the per-class NMS offset trick, all of
        #    which are easy to get subtly and invisibly wrong. Agent 6 owns its
        #    tests. A second copy would drift.
        if YOLO_DIR not in sys.path:
            sys.path.insert(0, YOLO_DIR)
        try:
            if YOLO_VARIANT in ("v26", "26", "yolo26"):
                import fpms_yolo26_npu as ynpu
            else:
                # "v8" imports fpms_yolo_npu.py, which exists ONLY on the old Pi
                # and is in no repository (fpms-selftest flags this). Kept so a
                # rover that still has that file works, but it is not the
                # supported path.
                import fpms_yolo_npu as ynpu       # noqa: F401
            self.decode = ynpu.decode
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            set_fault("decode_import",
                      f"cannot import the {YOLO_VARIANT} decode module from "
                      f"{YOLO_DIR}: {e}")
            return False

        # The module hardcodes an absolute MODEL path; honour FPMS_YOLO_DIR over
        # it so a relocated install does not load a stale model from elsewhere.
        model_name = os.path.basename(getattr(ynpu, "MODEL", "yolo26n-rk3588.rknn"))
        self.model_path = os.path.join(YOLO_DIR, model_name)

        # 2. The model file. NPU_SPEC.md design rule 5: "Do not guess a model.
        #    If the .rknn is absent or its checksum is unknown, that is a
        #    declared fault, not a silent fallback." The model is NOT in git -
        #    it existed only on the old Pi.
        if not os.path.exists(self.model_path):
            set_fault("model_missing",
                      f"{self.model_path} does not exist. The .rknn is not in "
                      f"the repository - it lived only on the old Pi. Nothing "
                      f"in git can rebuild it and the converter is x86-only.")
            return False
        try:
            self.model_bytes = os.path.getsize(self.model_path)
            self.model_sha = sha256_file(self.model_path)
        except OSError as e:
            set_fault("model_unreadable", f"{self.model_path}: {e}")
            return False

        # 3. Cross-check against agent 3's registry. Three distinct outcomes,
        #    deliberately NOT collapsed into one:
        #      - contradiction  -> refuse. A wrong-shaped or wrong-version model
        #                          loads fine and produces garbage boxes, which
        #                          is worse than no boxes.
        #      - no registry    -> serve, but publish sha_state=unverified. An
        #                          absent registry means agent 3's install has
        #                          not run; it is not evidence about the model.
        #      - agreement      -> serve, sha_state=verified.
        expected, note = registry_sha(self.model_path)
        self.sha_note = note
        if expected and expected != self.model_sha:
            self.sha_state = "mismatch"
            set_fault("model_sha_mismatch",
                      f"{self.model_path} is sha256 {self.model_sha[:16]}... but "
                      f"{MODELS_JSON} expects {expected[:16]}.... Refusing to "
                      f"serve: a wrong model produces boxes that look real.")
            return False
        self.sha_state = "verified" if expected else "unverified"

        # 4. The runtime. Imported lazily and guarded: rknn-toolkit-lite2 is an
        #    aarch64 Rockchip wheel that is NOT on PyPI, so on any dev machine
        #    this import fails and must fail as a named, published fault rather
        #    than as an ImportError at module scope that prevents the daemon
        #    from ever starting and publishing anything.
        try:
            from rknnlite.api import RKNNLite
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            set_fault("runtime_import",
                      f"rknnlite will not import ({e}). The rover will stream "
                      f"video and detect NOTHING. Install the aarch64 "
                      f"rknn-toolkit-lite2 wheel matched to librknnrt.so and to "
                      f"the kernel rknpu driver.")
            return False

        try:
            mask = self.resolve_core_mask(RKNNLite)
        except ValueError as e:
            set_fault("core_mask_unsupported", str(e))
            return False

        rknn = RKNNLite()
        try:
            if rknn.load_rknn(self.model_path) != 0:
                set_fault("runtime_load",
                          f"load_rknn returned non-zero for {self.model_path}")
                return False
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            set_fault("runtime_load", f"load_rknn raised for {self.model_path}: {e}")
            return False

        self.core_mask_fallback = False
        if not self._init_runtime(rknn, mask, RKNNLite):
            return False

        self.rknn = rknn

        # 5. The shape probe. Run ONE inference on a zeros frame before any
        #    client can be served.
        #
        #    Why at startup and not on the first real frame: a wrong output
        #    shape is permanent, and discovering it at 03:00 on the first frame
        #    that mattered means the rover was reporting healthy until then. A
        #    zeros image produces no detections, which is irrelevant - we are
        #    checking the TENSOR, not the content.
        #
        #    NOT a latency measurement. NPU_SPEC.md: "Measure sustained latency,
        #    not a first-inference number." probe_ms is published as a curiosity
        #    and must not be used as a budget.
        ok, why = self._probe()
        if not ok:
            self.release()
            set_fault("output_shape", why)
            return False

        self.loaded = True
        clear_fault()
        log(f"NPU ready: {self.model_path} sha256={self.model_sha[:16]}... "
            f"({self.model_bytes} bytes, {self.sha_state}: {self.sha_note}) "
            f"core_mask={self.core_mask_name}"
            f"{' [FELL BACK]' if self.core_mask_fallback else ''} "
            f"probe={self.probe_ms:.1f} ms")
        return True

    def _init_runtime(self, rknn, mask, rknnlite_cls):
        """init_runtime with the requested mask, with two honest fallbacks."""
        try:
            rc = rknn.init_runtime(core_mask=mask)
        except TypeError:
            # An older rknn-toolkit-lite2 has no core_mask keyword at all. Fall
            # back to the bare call and SAY SO - because the bare call is the
            # core-0-only behaviour that this whole layer exists to fix, and it
            # must not be able to hide behind a successful init.
            log("init_runtime() on this wheel does not accept core_mask; "
                "falling back to the default, which is CORE 0 ONLY")
            self.core_mask_name = "wheel-has-no-core_mask"
            self.core_mask_fallback = True
            try:
                rc = rknn.init_runtime()
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
                set_fault("runtime_init", f"init_runtime raised: {e}")
                return False
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            set_fault("runtime_init", f"init_runtime raised: {e}")
            return False

        if rc == 0:
            return True

        # A multi-core mask can be refused by a model that was not built for
        # it. Falling back to core 0 keeps detection alive, which is the right
        # trade - but it is REPORTED in telemetry as core_mask_fallback so the
        # degraded state is never mistaken for the configured one.
        single = getattr(rknnlite_cls, "NPU_CORE_0", None)
        if single is not None and self.core_mask_name not in (
                "0", "wheel-has-no-core_mask"):
            log(f"init_runtime(core_mask={self.core_mask_name}) returned {rc}; "
                f"retrying on core 0 only")
            try:
                rc = rknn.init_runtime(core_mask=single)
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
                set_fault("runtime_init",
                          f"init_runtime failed on {self.core_mask_name} (rc={rc}) "
                          f"and raised on the core-0 retry: {e}")
                return False
            if rc == 0:
                self.core_mask_name = "0"
                self.core_mask_fallback = True
                log("running on CORE 0 ONLY - two of three NPU cores are idle. "
                    "This model may not have been built for multi-core; see "
                    "npu/convert/ (agent 5) and fpms-npu-tune (agent 4).")
                return True

        set_fault("runtime_init",
                  f"init_runtime returned {rc}. This is the version-coupling "
                  f"failure: the kernel rknpu driver, /usr/lib/librknnrt.so and "
                  f"the rknn-toolkit-lite2 wheel MUST match. Compare "
                  f"{DRIVER_VERSION_PATH} against {NPU_VERSIONS_JSON}.")
        return False

    def _probe(self):
        blank = np.zeros((1, INPUT_SIZE, INPUT_SIZE, 3), dtype=np.uint8)
        t0 = time.perf_counter()
        try:
            self.inflight_started = time.monotonic()
            outputs = self.rknn.inference(inputs=[blank])
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            return False, f"the probe inference raised: {e}"
        finally:
            self.inflight_started = 0.0
        self.probe_ms = (time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000.0

        ok, why = validate_outputs(outputs)
        if not ok:
            return False, why
        try:
            self.decode(outputs, INPUT_SIZE, INPUT_SIZE)
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
            return False, (f"the decoder rejected the probe output: {e}. The "
                           f"model must be exported with end2end=false - "
                           f"end2end=true emits [1,300,6] and its top-k op "
                           f"segfaults on RK3588.")
        return True, ""

    def release(self):
        self.loaded = False
        if self.rknn is not None:
            try:
                self.rknn.release()
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
                log(f"rknn.release() raised (ignored): {e}")
        self.rknn = None


def validate_outputs(outputs):
    """Refuse anything that is not decodably [1,84,8400].

    NPU_SPEC.md pins the contract: input images [1,3,640,640], output output0
    [1,84,8400], 8400 = 80*80+40*40+20*20 anchors, 84 = 4 box + 80 class. RKNN
    is not consistent about whether the batch axis survives or whether the
    result comes back channel-first or channel-last, so this checks the SET of
    axes rather than a literal shape - the same reasoning as
    fpms_yolo26_npu._as_anchor_major(), which is where the decode itself
    handles the transpose.
    """
    if isinstance(outputs, (list, tuple)):
        if len(outputs) != 1:
            return False, (f"the model returned {len(outputs)} output tensors, "
                           f"expected exactly 1 (output0). A head split into "
                           f"separate branch tensors needs a different decoder; "
                           f"re-export as a single output0.")
        raw = outputs[0]
    else:
        raw = outputs
    try:
        a = np.squeeze(np.asarray(raw))
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        return False, f"the model output is not array-like: {e}"
    if a.ndim != 2 or set(a.shape) != {84, 8400}:
        return False, (f"output shape {np.asarray(raw).shape} is not [1,84,8400] "
                       f"(squeezed to {a.shape}). Refusing to decode it: a "
                       f"wrong-shaped tensor decodes into boxes that look real. "
                       f"Check FPMS_YOLO_VARIANT and the conversion recipe.")
    return True, ""


RT = Runtime()


# ------------------------------------------------------------ work queue ---


class Job:
    __slots__ = ("arr", "meta", "seq", "t_submit", "done", "reply")

    def __init__(self, arr, meta):
        self.arr = arr
        self.meta = meta
        self.seq = meta.get("seq")
        self.t_submit = time.monotonic()
        self.done = threading.Event()
        self.reply = None            # (msgtype, payload dict)

    def finish(self, msgtype, payload):
        # Set the reply BEFORE the event, or a client waking on the event can
        # read a None reply. Ordering, not locking, is what makes this safe.
        self.reply = (msgtype, payload)
        self.done.set()


QUEUE = collections.deque()
QUEUE_CV = threading.Condition()


def submit(job):
    """Enqueue, evicting the OLDEST job if full. See QUEUE in the docstring."""
    evicted = []
    with QUEUE_CV:
        while len(QUEUE) >= QUEUE_DEPTH:
            evicted.append(QUEUE.popleft())
        QUEUE.append(job)
        QUEUE_CV.notify()
    for old in evicted:
        # Answer it immediately. An evicted job left to time out would block a
        # client for the full deadline for no reason, and the client here is a
        # camera thread.
        with METRICS.lock:
            METRICS.drops += 1
        old.finish(MSG_DROPPED, {
            "seq": old.seq, "reason": "superseded",
            "queue_ms": round((time.monotonic() - old.t_submit) * 1000.0, 1),
            "detail": "a newer frame arrived while this one was still queued; "
                      "drop-oldest, so the boxes you keep are never stale",
        })


# ------------------------------------------------------------- inference ---


def run_job(job):
    """Run one inference. Worker thread only."""
    queue_ms = (time.monotonic() - job.t_submit) * 1000.0

    t0 = time.perf_counter()
    RT.inflight_started = time.monotonic()
    RT.inflight_seq = job.seq if isinstance(job.seq, int) else 0
    try:
        outputs = RT.rknn.inference(inputs=[job.arr])
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        with METRICS.lock:
            METRICS.errors += 1
            METRICS.consec_fail += 1
        set_fault("inference_error", f"rknn.inference raised: {e}")
        job.finish(MSG_UNAVAILABLE, {"seq": job.seq, "fault": "inference_error",
                                     "detail": str(e), "required": REQUIRED})
        return
    finally:
        RT.inflight_started = 0.0
    infer_ms = (time.perf_counter() - t0) * 1000.0

    # Shape is re-checked on EVERY inference, not just at load. It costs one
    # np.squeeze on a shape tuple. A model swapped underneath a running daemon,
    # or a driver that starts returning short tensors, must not reach the
    # decoder - and the decoder's own error would arrive as a generic exception
    # rather than as the named output_shape fault an operator can act on.
    ok, why = validate_outputs(outputs)
    if not ok:
        with METRICS.lock:
            METRICS.errors += 1
            METRICS.consec_fail += 1
        set_fault("output_shape", why)
        job.finish(MSG_UNAVAILABLE, {"seq": job.seq, "fault": "output_shape",
                                     "detail": why, "required": REQUIRED})
        return

    t1 = time.perf_counter()
    try:
        kw = {}
        if isinstance(job.meta.get("conf"), (int, float)):
            kw["conf_thres"] = float(job.meta["conf"])
        if isinstance(job.meta.get("iou"), (int, float)):
            kw["iou_thres"] = float(job.meta["iou"])
        boxes, scores, classes = RT.decode(outputs, int(job.meta["h"]),
                                           int(job.meta["w"]), **kw)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        with METRICS.lock:
            METRICS.errors += 1
            METRICS.consec_fail += 1
        set_fault("decode_error", f"decode raised: {e}")
        job.finish(MSG_UNAVAILABLE, {"seq": job.seq, "fault": "decode_error",
                                     "detail": str(e), "required": REQUIRED})
        return
    decode_ms = (time.perf_counter() - t1) * 1000.0

    METRICS.record(infer_ms, ok=True)
    with METRICS.lock:
        METRICS.consec_fail = 0
        if infer_ms > MAX_LATENCY_MS:
            METRICS.slow += 1
            METRICS.consec_slow += 1
        else:
            METRICS.consec_slow = 0
        slow_streak = METRICS.consec_slow

    if infer_ms > MAX_LATENCY_MS:
        # A single over-budget call is not yet a fault - RK3588 throttles and a
        # one-off excursion is expected. A STREAK is a fault, because that is
        # what "the NPU is no longer keeping up" looks like from here.
        if slow_streak >= REINIT_AFTER:
            set_fault("inference_slow",
                      f"{slow_streak} consecutive inferences over "
                      f"{MAX_LATENCY_MS:.0f} ms (last {infer_ms:.0f} ms). "
                      f"RK3588 throttles hard and silently under sustained "
                      f"load; check the thermal policy (agent 4).")
    elif current_fault() is not None:
        clear_fault()

    job.finish(MSG_RESULT, {
        "seq": job.seq,
        "n": int(len(boxes)),
        "boxes": [[round(float(v), 1) for v in b] for b in boxes],
        "scores": [round(float(s), 4) for s in scores],
        "classes": [int(c) for c in classes],
        "infer_ms": round(infer_ms, 2),
        "decode_ms": round(decode_ms, 2),
        "queue_ms": round(queue_ms, 2),
        "model_sha256": RT.model_sha,
        "core_mask": RT.core_mask_name,
    })


def worker_thread():
    """The ONLY thread that touches RKNNLite."""
    next_load_attempt = 0.0

    while RUNNING.is_set():
        if not RT.loaded and time.monotonic() >= next_load_attempt:
            # Loading happens HERE, not in main(), so the socket is already
            # listening while the model loads (seconds) and clients get an
            # honest UNAVAILABLE instead of ECONNREFUSED. Degrading is a state
            # we serve, not a state we disappear into.
            if not RT.load():
                # Keep retrying forever. A missing model may be copied across;
                # a driver may come back after a udev settle. Retrying costs a
                # sha256 of a few MB every RELOAD_BACKOFF_S and buys automatic
                # recovery without an operator noticing the unit needs a kick.
                next_load_attempt = time.monotonic() + RELOAD_BACKOFF_S

        with QUEUE_CV:
            while not QUEUE and RUNNING.is_set():
                QUEUE_CV.wait(0.25)
            if not RUNNING.is_set():
                break
            job = QUEUE.popleft()

        if not RT.loaded:
            f = current_fault() or {"fault": "unavailable",
                                    "detail": "runtime not loaded"}
            job.finish(MSG_UNAVAILABLE, {"seq": job.seq, "required": REQUIRED, **f})
            continue

        run_job(job)

        with METRICS.lock:
            need_reinit = METRICS.consec_fail >= REINIT_AFTER \
                or METRICS.consec_slow >= REINIT_AFTER * 2
        if need_reinit:
            # The soft recovery: tear the context down and build a new one, in
            # this same process. Legitimate for a call that RETURNED badly.
            # NOT legitimate for a call that never returned - see the watchdog.
            log("re-initialising the RKNN runtime after repeated failures")
            with METRICS.lock:
                METRICS.reinits += 1
                METRICS.consec_fail = 0
                METRICS.consec_slow = 0
            if not RT.load():
                next_load_attempt = time.monotonic() + RELOAD_BACKOFF_S

    RT.release()


# -------------------------------------------------------------- watchdog ---


def watchdog_thread():
    """Hard timeout + telemetry. 250 ms tick.

    Two jobs on one thread on purpose: both are periodic, neither is expensive,
    and a watchdog that shares a thread with the thing it watches would be
    useless - it shares with NEITHER the worker nor the connection threads.
    """
    hard_s = HARD_TIMEOUT_MS / 1000.0
    next_telemetry = 0.0

    while RUNNING.is_set():
        time.sleep(0.25)
        now = time.monotonic()

        started = RT.inflight_started
        if started and (now - started) > hard_s:
            age_ms = (now - started) * 1000.0
            log(f"WEDGED: rknn.inference() has been in flight for "
                f"{age_ms:.0f} ms (> {HARD_TIMEOUT_MS:.0f} ms). Exiting so "
                f"systemd can give us a fresh runtime.")
            BUS.publish("events/npu_fault", {
                "component": "npu", "alert": REQUIRED, "fault": "runtime_wedged",
                "error": f"rknn.inference() did not return within "
                         f"{HARD_TIMEOUT_MS:.0f} ms (seq {RT.inflight_seq}). "
                         f"The call is inside librknnrt.so and CANNOT be "
                         f"cancelled from Python; restarting the process is the "
                         f"only recovery. systemd will restart this unit.",
                "detection_available": False, "required": REQUIRED,
                "inflight_ms": round(age_ms, 1),
            }, qos=1)
            # Let paho's network thread drain the QoS-1 publish. 1.5 s is a
            # guess bounded by TimeoutStopSec=10 in the unit; better to lose
            # 1.5 s than to lose the one message that explains the restart.
            time.sleep(1.5)
            try:
                BUS.client.loop_stop()
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
                pass
            # os._exit, NOT sys.exit: sys.exit raises SystemExit in THIS thread
            # and the interpreter would then try to join the worker, which is
            # stuck in a C call and will never join. os._exit skips all of that
            # and hands the problem to systemd, which is the only component here
            # that can actually solve it.
            os._exit(3)

        if now >= next_telemetry:
            next_telemetry = now + TELEMETRY_S
            publish_telemetry()


def health_snapshot():
    m = METRICS.snapshot()
    f = current_fault()
    return {
        "component": "npu",
        "uptime_s": round(time.time() - STARTED, 1),
        "socket": SOCKET_PATH,
        "protocol": PROTO_VERSION,

        # Model
        "model_loaded": bool(RT.loaded),
        "model_path": RT.model_path,
        "model_sha256": RT.model_sha,
        "model_bytes": RT.model_bytes,
        "model_sha_state": RT.sha_state,      # verified | unverified | mismatch
        "model_sha_note": RT.sha_note,
        "variant": YOLO_VARIANT,

        # Cores. Requested vs in-force, always both, so a fallback is never
        # mistaken for the configuration.
        "core_mask_requested": CORE_MASK_NAME,
        "core_mask": RT.core_mask_name,
        "core_mask_fallback": RT.core_mask_fallback,

        # Latency. probe_ms is the FIRST-inference number and is published
        # separately from the percentiles precisely so it is not mistaken for
        # steady-state performance.
        "p50_ms": m["p50_ms"], "p90_ms": m["p90_ms"], "max_ms": m["max_ms"],
        "probe_ms": round(RT.probe_ms, 2) if RT.probe_ms else None,
        "budget_ms": MAX_LATENCY_MS,
        "hard_timeout_ms": HARD_TIMEOUT_MS,

        # Rates and counters
        "infer_per_s": m["infer_per_s"],
        "latency_samples": m["samples"],
        "inferences": m["total"],
        "drops": m["drops"],
        "timeouts": m["timeouts"],
        "slow": m["slow"],
        "errors": m["errors"],
        "consecutive_failures": m["consecutive_failures"],
        "consecutive_slow": m["consecutive_slow"],
        "reinits": m["reinits"],
        "protocol_errors": m["protocol_errors"],
        "queue_depth": QUEUE_DEPTH,
        "queued": len(QUEUE),
        "clients": CLIENTS["n"],

        # Versions. driver_version is read from debugfs, which is root-only by
        # default - a null here is expected under User=ubuntu and is NOT
        # evidence that the driver is missing. NPU_SPEC.md notes that nobody
        # ever recorded which version the old Pi ran; agent 1 writes the
        # build-time record that this compares against.
        "driver_version": read_text(DRIVER_VERSION_PATH),
        "npu_versions_recorded": os.path.exists(NPU_VERSIONS_JSON),

        # The one field a dashboard should key its "is detection real" light on.
        "detection_available": bool(RT.loaded) and f is None,
        "required": REQUIRED,
        "fault": (f or {}).get("fault"),
        "fault_detail": (f or {}).get("detail"),
        "fault_since": (f or {}).get("since"),
    }


def publish_telemetry():
    # QoS 0, on a timer, unconditionally - INCLUDING while faulted. A dashboard
    # must be able to distinguish "the NPU is broken" from "fpms-npud is not
    # running", and it can only do that if the broken case keeps talking.
    BUS.publish("telemetry/npu", health_snapshot(), qos=0)


# ----------------------------------------------------------- the socket ---

CLIENTS = {"n": 0}
CLIENTS_LOCK = threading.Lock()


def recv_exact(sock, n):
    buf = bytearray()
    while len(buf) < n:
        chunk = sock.recv(min(65536, n - len(buf)))
        if not chunk:
            raise ConnectionError("peer closed mid-frame")
        buf.extend(chunk)
    return bytes(buf)


def send_frame(sock, msgtype, payload):
    body = json.dumps(payload).encode("utf-8")
    sock.sendall(HEADER.pack(MAGIC, PROTO_VERSION, msgtype, 0, FMT_NONE,
                             len(body)) + body)


def graceful_close(sock):
    """Half-close, then drain to EOF, so a queued reply is not lost to an RST.

    MEASURED, on the first run of this daemon's protocol tests: closing a
    socket while unread bytes are still sitting in its receive buffer makes the
    kernel send RST instead of FIN, and an RST DISCARDS anything already
    written into the send buffer. The client then sees ConnectionResetError
    where we had just sent it a perfectly good PROTOCOL_ERROR naming the
    version mismatch.

    That would defeat the entire point of putting a version byte on the wire -
    the refusal would arrive as an anonymous reset, indistinguishable from a
    crashed daemon, and we would be back to guessing. So: shut down the write
    side (the peer gets our bytes plus EOF), then read whatever is still in
    flight and throw it away, then close.
    """
    try:
        sock.shutdown(socket.SHUT_WR)
    except OSError:
        return
    try:
        sock.settimeout(2.0)
        while sock.recv(65536):
            pass
    except OSError:
        pass


def refuse(sock, error, pending=0):
    """Answer a malformed frame with a NAMED PROTOCOL_ERROR, then close cleanly.

    `pending` is the payload the peer declared but we have not read. Draining
    it first is what makes the reply survive the close - see graceful_close().
    A bogus over-cap length is passed as 0, because draining a length we have
    already decided not to trust would be doing the attacker's allocation for
    them.
    """
    with METRICS.lock:
        METRICS.proto_errors += 1
    if 0 < pending <= MAX_PAYLOAD:
        try:
            recv_exact(sock, pending)
        except (OSError, ConnectionError):
            pass
    try:
        send_frame(sock, MSG_PROTOCOL_ERROR,
                   {"error": error, "daemon_version": PROTO_VERSION})
    except OSError:
        pass
    graceful_close(sock)


def client_thread(sock, addr):
    # A generous but finite socket timeout. Without it a client that connects
    # and never speaks pins a thread forever, and MAX_CLIENTS then becomes a
    # denial of service against ourselves rather than a protection.
    sock.settimeout(30.0)

    # Strictly greater than the worker's budget - see TIMEOUTS in the docstring.
    deadline_s = (MAX_LATENCY_MS * 2.0) / 1000.0

    try:
        while RUNNING.is_set():
            try:
                head = recv_exact(sock, HEADER_LEN)
            except (socket.timeout, ConnectionError, OSError):
                return

            magic, ver, msgtype, flags, fmt, length = HEADER.unpack(head)

            if length > MAX_PAYLOAD:
                # Checked FIRST and without draining: a bogus length prefix is
                # the one error where reading what the peer claims to have sent
                # is itself the attack.
                refuse(sock, f"payload {length} exceeds the {MAX_PAYLOAD} byte "
                             f"cap; refused before any allocation")
                return
            if magic != MAGIC:
                refuse(sock, "bad magic; this is not an fpms-npud frame stream",
                       length)
                return
            if ver != PROTO_VERSION:
                # THE reason there is a version byte. A mismatch is NAMED and
                # the connection is dropped, rather than a struct that happens
                # to unpack and a box array that happens to parse.
                refuse(sock, f"protocol version {ver} != {PROTO_VERSION}; "
                             f"client and daemon are from different builds",
                       length)
                return
            if flags != 0:
                refuse(sock, f"flags={flags} is reserved and must be 0; this "
                             f"daemon does not understand it and will not "
                             f"silently ignore it", length)
                return

            payload = recv_exact(sock, length) if length else b""

            if msgtype == MSG_HEALTH:
                send_frame(sock, MSG_HEALTH_OK, health_snapshot())
                continue

            if msgtype != MSG_INFER:
                refuse(sock, f"unknown msgtype 0x{msgtype:02x}")
                return

            ok, parsed = parse_infer(fmt, payload)
            if not ok:
                refuse(sock, parsed)
                return
            arr, meta = parsed

            # Answer before queueing when we already know we cannot serve. No
            # point occupying the single queue slot with a job whose only
            # possible outcome is a refusal.
            if not RT.loaded:
                f = current_fault() or {"fault": "unavailable",
                                        "detail": "runtime not loaded"}
                send_frame(sock, MSG_UNAVAILABLE,
                           {"seq": meta.get("seq"), "required": REQUIRED, **f})
                continue

            job = Job(arr, meta)
            submit(job)

            if not job.done.wait(deadline_s):
                # The enforceable timeout. We give up on the reply; the worker
                # may still be inside librknnrt and we cannot stop it. What we
                # CAN guarantee is that the client - a camera thread - gets its
                # thread back on schedule, every time.
                with METRICS.lock:
                    METRICS.timeouts += 1
                send_frame(sock, MSG_UNAVAILABLE, {
                    "seq": meta.get("seq"), "fault": "timeout",
                    "detail": f"no result within {deadline_s * 1000:.0f} ms "
                              f"(2x the {MAX_LATENCY_MS:.0f} ms budget). The "
                              f"inference may still be running; it cannot be "
                              f"cancelled from Python.",
                    "since": time.time(), "required": REQUIRED})
                continue

            msgtype_out, body = job.reply
            send_frame(sock, msgtype_out, body)

    except (BrokenPipeError, ConnectionResetError):
        pass
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"client {addr or 'unix'} error: {e}")
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        log(f"client handler crashed: {e}")
    finally:
        try:
            sock.close()
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
            pass
        with CLIENTS_LOCK:
            CLIENTS["n"] -= 1


def parse_infer(fmt, payload):
    """(True, (arr, meta)) or (False, "why")."""
    if fmt != FMT_RGB888_640:
        return False, (f"fmt 0x{fmt:02x} is not supported; this daemon accepts "
                       f"only 0x01 RGB888_640 (640x640x3 uint8 RGB, HWC, "
                       f"C-contiguous)")
    if len(payload) < 4:
        return False, "payload too short for the meta length prefix"
    (meta_len,) = struct.unpack_from("!I", payload, 0)
    if 4 + meta_len > len(payload):
        return False, f"meta_len {meta_len} runs past the end of the payload"
    try:
        meta = json.loads(payload[4:4 + meta_len].decode("utf-8"))
    except (UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError) as e:
        return False, f"meta is not UTF-8 JSON: {e}"
    if not isinstance(meta, dict):
        return False, "meta must be a JSON object"

    img = payload[4 + meta_len:]
    if len(img) != IMAGE_BYTES:
        return False, (f"image is {len(img)} bytes, expected exactly "
                       f"{IMAGE_BYTES} for 640x640x3 uint8")
    try:
        h, w = int(meta["h"]), int(meta["w"])
    except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError):
        return False, ("meta must carry integer \"h\" and \"w\" - the ORIGINAL "
                       "frame height and width. Boxes are rescaled into that "
                       "space with an independent per-axis ratio, because the "
                       "preprocess is a stretch resize and not a letterbox.")
    if not (0 < h <= 8192 and 0 < w <= 8192):
        return False, f"implausible original frame size {w}x{h}"

    # .copy() rather than a read-only frombuffer view, on purpose. It costs a
    # ~1.2 MB memcpy (sub-millisecond, against a ~31 ms inference) and buys a
    # writable, aligned, C-contiguous buffer that WE own for the whole duration
    # of a C call we cannot interrupt. The alternative hands librknnrt a view
    # onto a bytes object whose lifetime is tied to a socket read - which is
    # fine right up until a refactor makes it not fine, and that failure would
    # be a segfault, not an exception.
    arr = np.frombuffer(img, dtype=np.uint8).reshape(
        1, INPUT_SIZE, INPUT_SIZE, 3).copy()
    return True, (arr, meta)


def bind_socket():
    path = SOCKET_PATH
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path) or "/", exist_ok=True)
    except OSError as e:
        log(f"could not create {os.path.dirname(path)}: {e}")

    # A crash leaves the inode behind and bind() then fails with EADDRINUSE
    # forever. Unlink first - but ONLY if nothing is listening on it, so a
    # second copy of the daemon cannot silently steal the socket from the
    # first and leave two processes fighting over one NPU.
    if os.path.exists(path):
        probe = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
        probe.settimeout(0.5)
        live = False
        try:
            probe.connect(path)
            live = True
        except OSError:
            # ECONNREFUSED / ENOTSOCK / ENOENT all mean the same thing here:
            # whatever is at that path, nobody is accepting on it.
            pass
        finally:
            try:
                probe.close()
            except OSError:
                pass
        if live:
            raise SystemExit(
                f"{path} already has a listener - another fpms-npud is "
                f"running. Refusing to start a second owner of the NPU: two "
                f"processes racing for the same NPU cores is a failure nobody "
                f"can debug from a rover.")
        os.unlink(path)
        log(f"removed a stale socket at {path}")

    srv = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX, socket.SOCK_STREAM)
    srv.bind(path)
    # 0660: owner (ubuntu) and group only. The agent runs as ubuntu:ubuntu, so
    # this is exactly the access it needs and nothing else on the box gets to
    # queue work onto the NPU. A world-writable IPC socket on a rover is a
    # /cmd_vel-shaped mistake in a different costume.
    os.chmod(path, 0o660)
    srv.listen(16)
    srv.settimeout(0.5)          # so the accept loop can observe RUNNING
    return srv


def accept_loop(srv):
    while RUNNING.is_set():
        try:
            conn, addr = srv.accept()
        except socket.timeout:
            continue
        except OSError:
            if RUNNING.is_set():
                log("accept failed; the listening socket is gone")
            return

        with CLIENTS_LOCK:
            if CLIENTS["n"] >= MAX_CLIENTS:
                # Refuse politely and name the reason. A silent close here
                # would look to the agent exactly like a dead daemon.
                try:
                    send_frame(conn, MSG_UNAVAILABLE, {
                        "fault": "too_many_clients",
                        "detail": f"{CLIENTS['n']} connections already open "
                                  f"(FPMS_NPU_MAX_CLIENTS={MAX_CLIENTS}); this "
                                  f"usually means a client is leaking sockets",
                        "since": time.time(), "required": REQUIRED})
                except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
                    pass
                # Half-close and drain: the client may already have pushed a
                # 1.2 MB frame at us, and closing on top of it would replace
                # this explanation with a bare RST.
                graceful_close(conn)
                conn.close()
                continue
            CLIENTS["n"] += 1

        threading.Thread(target=client_thread, args=(conn, addr),
                         daemon=True).start()


# -------------------------------------------------------------- lifecycle ---


def on_signal(signum, _frame):
    log(f"signal {signum}; shutting down")
    RUNNING.clear()
    with QUEUE_CV:
        QUEUE_CV.notify_all()


def main():
    if "--help" in sys.argv or "-h" in sys.argv:
        print(__doc__)
        return 0

    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, on_signal)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, on_signal)

    if not os.path.exists("/etc/fpms/config.env"):
        # Not fatal, but it is the single most common cause of "every service
        # is active and the dashboard is empty" in this project: every
        # load_config() swallows the absence and falls back to a broker nobody
        # is running. Say it out loud.
        log("WARNING: /etc/fpms/config.env is absent; running on built-in "
            "defaults. Broker credentials will almost certainly be wrong and "
            "nothing will crash to tell you. Run: sudo fpms-firstboot --force")

    log(f"fpms-npud protocol v{PROTO_VERSION} starting: socket={SOCKET_PATH} "
        f"variant={YOLO_VARIANT} dir={YOLO_DIR} core_mask={CORE_MASK_NAME} "
        f"budget={MAX_LATENCY_MS:.0f}ms hard={HARD_TIMEOUT_MS:.0f}ms "
        f"queue={QUEUE_DEPTH} required={REQUIRED}")

    BUS.start()

    try:
        srv = bind_socket()
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except OSError as e:
        # Cannot serve at all. Publish it before dying, so the failure is on a
        # topic and not only in a journal, then let systemd restart us.
        set_fault("socket_bind", f"cannot bind {SOCKET_PATH}: {e}")
        time.sleep(1.0)
        return 4
    log(f"listening on {SOCKET_PATH} (mode 0660)")

    threading.Thread(target=worker_thread, name="npu-worker", daemon=True).start()
    threading.Thread(target=watchdog_thread, name="npu-watchdog", daemon=True).start()

    try:
        accept_loop(srv)
    finally:
        RUNNING.clear()
        with QUEUE_CV:
            QUEUE_CV.notify_all()
        try:
            srv.close()
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
            pass
        try:
            os.unlink(SOCKET_PATH)
        except OSError:
            pass
        BUS.publish("events/npu_offline", {
            "component": "npu", "alert": False, "reason": "clean shutdown",
            "detection_available": False}, qos=1)
        # Give paho a moment to drain the QoS-1 goodbye, then stop cleanly so
        # the last will does NOT also fire - a clean stop must not look like a
        # crash on the dashboard.
        time.sleep(0.5)
        try:
            BUS.client.loop_stop()
            BUS.client.disconnect()
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
            pass
        # The worker may be inside a C call we cannot interrupt. It is a daemon
        # thread, so the interpreter will not wait for it; TimeoutStopSec=10 in
        # the unit is the real bound, and SIGKILL after that is acceptable
        # because this process holds no state anyone needs preserved.
    log("stopped")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
